### Steps to reproduce

With `Hyphens.manual` (the default since #185152), lay out text whose line breaks at a soft hyphen (U+00AD) when the line would not fit with the hyphen added, but an earlier break opportunity exists on the same line.

### Expected results

The hyphen fits within the paragraph width. Browsers count the hyphen when choosing a soft-hyphen break: if the break does not fit with its hyphen, they break at the previous opportunity on the line instead, such as a space or an earlier soft hyphen. In Chrome (`hyphens: manual`), the example below gives the lines "ab" and "cdef". Browsers let the hyphen overflow only when the soft hyphen is the first break opportunity on the line, so splitting the word is the only option.

### Actual results

The lines are "ab cd-" and "ef", and `longestLine` is 60, wider than the 55 constraint. The hyphen is drawn past the paragraph width and paints outside the `Text` widget's bounds, unclipped. `TextPainter.width` and the `RenderParagraph` size stay at 55, so Flutter's overflow handling does not see it. With real fonts this is a few pixels:

<img width="640" height="240" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/a92f423f-816a-4655-99f7-dac955aaab09" />

The same text in Georgia at 22px in a 226px box; the red line is the box edge. Flutter draws "incompre-" past the edge. Chrome breaks at the earlier soft hyphen instead ("incom-").

### Code sample

```dart
import 'dart:ui' as ui;

import 'package:flutter_test/flutter_test.dart';

void main() {
  test('the rendered soft hyphen fits within the paragraph width', () {
    // With the FlutterTest font at 10px, every glyph, including the space, is 10 wide.
    final ui.ParagraphBuilder builder = ui.ParagraphBuilder(
      ui.ParagraphStyle(fontFamily: 'FlutterTest', fontSize: 10, hyphens: ui.Hyphens.manual),
    )..addText('ab cd\u00ADef');
    final ui.Paragraph paragraph = builder.build()
      ..layout(const ui.ParagraphConstraints(width: 55));

    expect(paragraph.longestLine, lessThanOrEqualTo(55)); // Fails: 60.0
  });
}
```

### Cause

The layout comes from Skia's skparagraph. `TextWrapper::lookAhead` chooses the break without counting the hyphen, and `TextLine::createSoftHyphen` adds the hyphen after the line is built, so the framework cannot correct it after layout. A fix in the skparagraph line breaker is in review: https://skia-review.googlesource.com/c/skia/+/1397276.

### Flutter version

master at a3d71d82160 (2026-10-16).

### Steps to reproduce

1. Put an `Image` that has a `frameBuilder` under a `TickerMode`. Use the same `Image` instance in each pump, so that only `TickerMode` can rebuild it.
2. After the image loads, set `TickerMode.enabled` to false, and then to true again.
3. Look at the number of builds, and at the `frame` and `wasSynchronouslyLoaded` values that `frameBuilder` gets.

The code sample below does these steps with `flutter test`.

### Expected results

- Changing `TickerMode` does not rebuild the `Image`. `TickerMode` only has to pause and resume the image stream.
- For a single-frame image, `frame` stays 0 and `wasSynchronouslyLoaded` does not change. `ImageFrameBuilder` documents that for single-frame images `frame` "will never be greater than zero", and that `wasSynchronouslyLoaded` "will be the same for all image frames".

### Actual results

With a 1x1 PNG:

| | Rebuilds since load | `frame` | `wasSynchronouslyLoaded` |
|---|---:|---:|---|
| After load | 0 | 0 | false |
| After `TickerMode` off | 1 | 0 | false |
| After `TickerMode` on | 2 | 1 | true |

Each off and on adds 1 to `frame`.

There are two causes:

- `_ImageState.didChangeDependencies` calls `TickerMode.of(context)`, so the `Image` depends on `TickerMode`. Each change rebuilds it and calls `_resolveImage` again.
- While animations are paused, the `Image` does not listen to its image stream. When they resume, it listens again, and the stream sends the frame that is already shown. `_handleImageFrame` counts this frame as a new frame and rebuilds.

In a desktop app, opening a search page turns `TickerMode` off for the page under it. This rebuilds 114 `Image` widgets in one frame (about 1.2 ms of build time, Linux, profile mode).

