Gemini found this and wrote / ran the test. **Low Priority**: this likely only affects tests since most font managers will fail to match `""`.

### Description
`TextStyle.fontFamily` [documents](https://main-api.flutter.dev/flutter/painting/TextStyle/fontFamily.html) that 
> When [fontFamily](https://main-api.flutter.dev/flutter/painting/TextStyle/fontFamily.html) is null or not provided, the first value in [fontFamilyFallback](https://main-api.flutter.dev/flutter/painting/TextStyle/fontFamilyFallback.html) acts as the preferred/first font family. When neither is provided, then the default platform font will be used.

However, `ParagraphBuilder.pushStyle` unconditionally prepends "" when `fontFamily` is null, passing `["", ...fontFamilyFallback]` to `txt::TextStyle::font_families` (unlike `StrutStyle`, which only pushes fontFamily when non-null).

### Minimal Repro
```dart
import 'package:flutter/painting.dart';
import 'package:flutter_test/flutter_test.dart';

void main() {
  test('fontFamilyFallback[0] is used as preferred font when fontFamily is null', () {
    Size measureSize(TextStyle style) {
      final painter = TextPainter(
        text: TextSpan(text: 'Hello', style: style),
        textDirection: TextDirection.ltr,
      )..layout();
      return painter.size;
    }

    // 'Cough' is a built-in test font embedded in flutter_tester alongside 'FlutterTest'.
    final Size withFontFamily = measureSize(
      const TextStyle(fontFamily: 'Cough', fontSize: 100),
    );
    final Size withFallbackOnly = measureSize(
      const TextStyle(fontFamilyFallback: <String>['Cough'], fontSize: 100),
    );

    expect(withFallbackOnly, equals(withFontFamily));
  });
}
```
### Expected Behavior
`txt::TextStyle::font_families` is ["Roboto", "Noto"], with "Roboto" as the preferred font family.

### Actual Behavior
`txt::TextStyle::font_families` is ["", "Roboto", "Noto"], causing SkParagraph to query matchFamily("") across all font managers before "Roboto".

### Steps to reproduce

This is the iOS-engine side of #190357. The framework side (a Material `Slider` on a pushed route makes the framework reference a semantics node that the engine never received) is tracked there and addressed by PR #193372. On iOS, though, such a dangling child id does not just produce a bad element: `AccessibilityBridge::GetOrCreateObject` turns it into an **empty root node**, and the whole accessibility tree is gone until the app is restarted. Any other source of a dangling child id would do the same, so this seems worth fixing in the engine regardless of #193372.

1. Run the app below on an iOS device or simulator.
2. Turn on VoiceOver, or inspect the app with Accessibility Inspector / XCUITest (`/source` in WebDriverAgent).
3. Tap **Open**, drag the slider a few times (on a real screen: scroll a list that contains the slider), go back.

<details><summary>main.dart</summary>

