### Expected results

A child id that the bridge does not know is ignored (or becomes one missing element). The rest of the accessibility tree stays intact.

### Actual results

On device, at some point the accessibility tree becomes empty: XCUITest/WebDriverAgent see only `Application → Window → Other` with no children, and VoiceOver has nothing to focus. The tree stays empty on every screen until the app is restarted. We hit this in a production app (iPhone 16 Pro, iOS 26, release build) on a settings screen with two sliders: intact at the top of the screen, empty after scrolling.

It is probabilistic, because it depends on `std::unordered_map` iteration order (see below). The framework half is deterministic: a widget test that records `updateNode` calls and replays them under the bridge's rule (only nodes reachable from the root survive) reports `update 4: #9 -> #6` on 3.47.3 for the app above: slider row `#9` lists `#6` in `childrenInTraversalOrder`, and `#6` is neither in that update nor in the bridge. This is the
