    );
    for (final r in tester.allRenderObjects.whereType<RenderParagraph>()) {
      // ignore: invalid_use_of_protected_member
      debugPrint('${r.text.toPlainText()} ${r.text.style?.leadingDistribution} baseline ${r.computeDistanceToActualBaseline(TextBaseline.alphabetic)}');
    }
  });
}
```

### Expected results

The second `Hello` uses proportional leading, so its baseline is 16.875, the same as `Hello!`.

### Actual results

```
Hello TextLeadingDistribution.even baseline 14.5
Hello null baseline 14.5
Hello! null baseline 16.875
```

The second `Hello` gets the first one's `even` layout. Changing the text by one character (`Hello!`) avoids the cache and lays it out correctly. In a real app, text jumps by a couple of pixels while the user types, depending on what was laid out before. We see it on web (CanvasKit) and on native macOS, with or without Impeller.

### Cause

SkParagraph's `ParagraphCache` compares text styles with `TextStyle::equalsByFonts` (`modules/skparagraph/src/TextStyle.cpp`). That checks height, font size, families and features, but not `fHalfLeading`, and the key's hash in `ParagraphCache.cpp` leaves it out as well. On a cache hit, `ParagraphCache::updateTo` copies the cached runs, and each run fixed its half-leading choice when it was shaped (`Run::fUseHalfLeading`). The code is the same in the Skia revision Flutter 3.44.8 uses (`e9ed4fc9`) and on Skia `main`.

### Workaround

Don't render the same text with styles that differ only in `leadingDistribution`.

