### Steps to reproduce

1. Create a `RenderTarget` with two color attachments of different formats, for example `r16g16b16a16Float` at index 0 and `r8g8b8a8UNormInt` at index 1.
2. Bind a pipeline whose fragment shader writes both `layout(location = 0)` and `layout(location = 1)`, and draw a triangle.
3. Submit and read attachment 1 back.

`RenderPass::GetOrCreatePipeline` (`lib/gpu/render_pass.cc`) fills in the color attachment formats of the pipeline descriptor like this:

```cpp
render_target_.IterateAllColorAttachments(
    [&](size_t index, const impeller::ColorAttachment& attachment) -> bool {
      auto& color = ColorAttachmentDescriptorAt(index);
      color.format = render_target_.GetRenderTargetPixelFormat();
      return true;
    });
```

`RenderTarget::GetRenderTargetPixelFormat()` returns the format of attachment 0.

### Expected results

Each color attachment of the pipeline has its own texture's format, and the triangle is drawn into both attachments.

### Actual results

The pipeline is built as if every attachment had attachment 0's format, so it doesn't match its render pass, and nothing reports it. On Metal (macOS, `flutter_tester`) attachment 1 stays empty after the draw. On Vulkan the pipeline and the render pass are incompatible by the spec; SwiftShader renders correctly anyway, so other drivers may behave differently.

A related problem in the same loop: the pipeline gets every color descriptor the pass has stored, including ones for indices with no attachment. `setColorBlendEnable(true, colorAttachmentIndex: 1)` on a one-attachment target adds an attachment with an unknown format to the pipeline.

(Separately, the OpenGL ES backend aborts in debug with "Color attachment is too complicated for a legacy renderer" when a render target has more than one color attachment. That's a different limitation, and I'm keeping it out of this issue.)

### Suggested fix

Use `attachment.texture->GetTextureDescriptor().format` for each attachment instead of the render target's attachment 0 format, put only indices that have an attachment into the pipeline, and build the descriptors on a copy, so building a pipeline doesn't write formats back into the stored blend state.

