### Steps to reproduce

`fml::Status` (`engine/src/flutter/fml/status.h`) stores its message as a `std::string_view`:

```cpp
class Status final {
  ...
 private:
  fml::StatusCode code_;
  std::string_view message_;
};
```

The constructor takes a `std::string_view`, so passing a `std::string` compiles without a warning, and the status then points into that string's buffer.

1. Build a status from a `std::string`.
2. Change or destroy the string.
3. Read `status.message()`.

There is one such caller on main today, in `impeller/renderer/backend/vulkan/command_queue_vk.cc` (`CommandQueueVK::Submit`):

```cpp
auto submit_callback = [&context, &vk_buffers](vk::Fence submit_fence) {
  ...
  if (status != vk::Result::eSuccess) {
    std::string submit_error =
        "Failed to submit command: " + vk::to_string(status);
    VALIDATION_LOG << submit_error;
    return fml::Status(fml::StatusCode::kCancelled, submit_error);
  }
  return fml::Status();
};
```

`FenceWaiterVK::AddFence` returns this status, and `CommandQueueVK::Submit` hands it to its own caller after `submit_error` is gone.

### Expected results

`message()` returns the message the status was built with, for as long as the status lives.

### Actual results

`message()` returns whatever the string's buffer holds now: `"secon"` after the string was changed to `"second"`, or freed memory after it was destroyed. When a Vulkan queue submit fails, anyone who reads the returned `status.message()` reads freed heap memory: garbage in a log, a crash under ASan. Every other caller in the tree passes a string literal, so nothing goes wrong until someone formats a message at runtime.

### Suggested fix

Store the message as a `std::string`. `message()` keeps returning `std::string_view`, so no caller has to change. It costs one copy of the message when a non-OK status is created; OK statuses have an empty message and don't allocate. I'd rather fix the type than the one caller, because the next caller that formats a message would fall into the same trap.

