### Use case

Flutter GPU builds a pipeline inside the first `RenderPass.draw` that uses it, and the calling thread waits for the compile ([`RenderPass::GetOrCreatePipeline`](https://github.com/flutter/flutter/blob/d7fe60992f6c9e35d56169c68ad15d4e56ab9d84/engine/src/flutter/lib/gpu/render_pass.cc#L180-L268)). For a game engine this means a frame stalls whenever a new shader variant first draws. In flutter_scene on a Galaxy A16 (Mali-G57), the first draw of two lit pipelines costs a frame of about 1.1 s, and a warm pipeline cache does not help. There is no way to build a pipeline ahead of time without drawing, or to check whether one is ready.

### Proposal

Let apps prepare a pipeline for a render target layout and fixed-function state, built off the calling thread.

```dart
PreparedPipeline prepareRenderPipeline(
  RenderPipeline pipeline, {
  required RenderTargetLayout target, // color formats, depth/stencil format, sample count
  RenderPipelineState state,          // blend, depth, stencil, cull, winding, primitive
});

final class PreparedPipeline {
  bool get isReady;        // never blocks
  Future<void> get ready;  // completes when built
}
```

An engine can then compile during loading and keep drawing a pipeline that is already built until a new one is ready. `draw` keeps its current behavior. Impeller's `PipelineLibrary::GetPipeline(descriptor, async = true)` already returns a future, so most of the plumbing exists.

