Deployed repro: https://flutter-demo-77-before.web.app

### Expected results

After the finger lifts, "Pointers down" is 0 and the list is idle.

### Actual results

"Pointers down" stays at 1 and the list never returns to idle. Flutter never receives the pointer up for that drag, so it keeps the pointer down for the rest of the session. A later drag on the list does not clear it either.

### Cause

On mobile, the engine covers each view with an `flt-semantics-placeholder` that waits for a screen reader to activate it. In `MobileSemanticsEnabler.tryEnableSemantics`, in `lib/web_ui/lib/src/engine/semantics/semantics_helper.dart`, the engine gives up after `kMaxSemanticsActivationAttempts`, 20 pointer and touch events, and schedules the placeholder for removal.

On WebKit, the placeholder is only removed on `touchend`, `pointerup`, or `click`, so that Safari does not swallow the rest of a gesture whose target was removed. That check was added in flutter/engine#22965. On every other browser engine, the placeholder is removed on the very next event, which is usually a `pointermove` in the middle of a drag.

The placeholder is the target of that drag. Once it is removed, Chrome on Android no longer delivers the drag's remaining pointer events, including `pointerup`, to the window when the page allows panning and `touchmove` is canceled. The engine listens for `pointerup` on the window, so the framework never sees the pointer go up.

Without the `index.html` block, with the engine's default `touch-action: none`, Chrome still delivers the `pointerup` and the drag ends normally. A default Flutter app does not hit this, but any page that scrolls itself, which is what #69529 asks for, does.

