## Summary

For each HostApi, pigeon asks jnigen to bind both the Kotlin interface (`FooApi`) and its Registrar (`FooApiRegistrar`). The generated Dart only ever calls the Registrar, so the interface's bindings are dead code. They are the single largest part of the generated `.jni.dart` file.

## Details

- [jnigen_config_generator.dart L127–132](https://github.com/flutter/packages/blob/e18a54144e0541b9bfbc4b7cb9837bc224d9bbb7/packages/pigeon/lib/src/kotlin/jnigen_config_generator.dart#L127-L132) adds `'${api.name}'` and `'${api.name}Registrar'` to the jnigen class list for every HostApi and FlutterApi.
- The generated Dart holds and calls only the Registrar ([native_interop_tests.gen.dart L2707](https://github.com/flutter/packages/blob/e18a54144e0541b9bfbc4b7cb9837bc224d9bbb7/packages/pigeon/platform_tests/shared_test_plugin_code/lib/src/generated/native_interop_tests.gen.dart#L2707)). Nothing in it references the bound HostApi interface.
- The **FlutterApi** interface is different: Dart implements it, so its binding is needed and should stay.

## Measurements

From pigeon's test plugin, regenerated with jnigen 1.0.1 and formatted at width 100:

| Class list | `.jni.dart` lines |
| :--- | ---: |
| Today | 40,948 |
| Without the HostApi interface | 25,507 (−37.7%) |

- The HostApi interface's bindings are 14,438 lines, 35.2% of the file.
- Without it, jnigen emits a 6-line stub for the Registrar's supertype, and the Registrar's own bindings come out **byte-identical**.
- The result passes the analyzer, and all 25 native interop probe calls pass in a release build.

## Proposed fix

List only the Registrar for HostApis:

```dart
for (final Api api in root.apis) {
  if (api is AstHostApi) {
    indent.writeln("'$packagePrefix${api.name}Registrar',");
  } else if (api is AstFlutterApi) {
    indent.writeln("'$packagePrefix${api.name}',");
    indent.writeln("'$packagePrefix${api.name}Registrar',");
  }
}
```

The Kotlin interface stays as it is: the Registrar implements it, and users implement it. Only its Dart binding goes away. This is a patch-level change; plugins pick it up the next time they regenerate.

**Benefits:** smaller generated Dart (faster analysis, less code to ship and review) and less work for jnigen. No API or behavior change.

