### Steps to reproduce

1. Take a large asset with thin, high-contrast details, e.g. a 1024×1024 PNG logo.
2. Display it at ~120 logical pixels twice: once plain, once with `cacheWidth` set to the display size in physical pixels (the usage recommended for saving memory).
3. Compare the two.

### Expected results

Both images look the same (or nearly so). `cacheWidth` should only reduce memory, since the image is never drawn larger than the cache size.

### Actual results

The `cacheWidth` version is visibly aliased: thin lines and curved edges are jagged ("pixelated"). The plain version, minified on the GPU with mipmaps (`FilterQuality.medium`), is smooth.

This was reported by users of our app: https://github.com/Otzaria/otzaria/issues/1747 (logo decoded from 1024 px to ~120 px).

### Cause

The decode-time resize uses bilinear sampling without mipmaps, so for large reduction ratios (~8× here) each output pixel samples only 2×2 of the ~8×8 source pixels it covers:

- `engine/src/flutter/lib/ui/painting/image_decoder_impeller.cc` (`ResizeOnCpu`):
  `scalePixels(..., SkSamplingOptions(SkFilterMode::kLinear, SkMipmapMode::kNone))`
- `engine/src/flutter/lib/ui/painting/image_decoder_skia.cc` (`ResizeRasterImage`): same sampling options.
- The Impeller GPU path (`blit_pass->ResizeTexture(dest_texture, resize_texture)`) also resizes from the non-mipmapped source; mipmaps are generated only for the destination.

Possible fixes: use mipmapped sampling (`SkMipmapMode::kLinear`) or `SkCubicResampler` for the CPU path, or downscale in successive halving steps / generate source mipmaps before the GPU resize, at least when the reduction ratio is greater than 2.

This is the same problem as #56239, which was closed by #184549. That PR adds logical-pixel sizing to `ResizeImage` but does not change the resize quality, so the original quality issue is still present.

