flutter build windows --debug, then run build\windows\x64\runner\Debug\postplatformthreadtask_uaf.exe directly.
About ten seconds after the window appears, the process crashes with an access violation (exit code 0xC0000005 / -1073741819) while servicing the posted task — the task body ran line never prints (cdb capture in Logs; the delay is generous so flutter run can attach first — with a shorter delay flutter run may instead report "App failed to start" because the app dies before the tool attaches, which is the same crash).

### Expected results

Per the API contract documented in `flutter_windows.h`:

```cpp
// If the task is discarded without being executed (e.g. during engine
// shutdown), |on_cancel| is called on the platform thread so the caller can
// cleanup allocations. |on_cancel| can be nullptr if no cleanup is needed.
```

`on_cancel` should only ever run if a task is discarded without executing — never at post time, and never on the posting thread. In the repro, the task body should run on valid memory.

### Actual results

- `on_cancel(user_data)` is invoked synchronously, on the posting thread, inside the `FlutterDesktopEnginePostPlatformThreadTask` call.
- The task is still queued and executed. With any `on_cancel` that releases `user_data` — the documented cleanup contract, and exactly what the shipped `cpp_client_wrapper`'s `FlutterEngine::PostPlatformThreadTask` passes (`delete user_data`, `cpp_client_wrapper/flutter_engine.cc`) — the platform thread then invokes `callback` on freed memory. **Every task posted through the official wrapper is a use-after-free, including on current master.**
- If the engine shuts down before the task runs, the heap-side `Context` destructor calls `on_cancel` a second time → double free.

