## Summary

With a `flutter build web --wasm` (skwasm) build, an `HtmlElementView` whose platform-view factory returns an empty container div — into which content (a real third-party `<iframe>`) is appended *later*, after an async gap — silently fails to ever show that content when the page is running under real GPU-accelerated rendering. No console error, no exception, nothing in `Network` — the container div exists, but stays empty forever. The exact same build works correctly when Chrome is forced to `--disable-gpu` (software rendering), which is what makes this easy to miss in CI/headless testing that defaults to software rendering.

Concretely, this breaks Google Identity Services' "Sign in with Google" button (`google_identity_services_web`'s `gis.id.renderButton()`, which injects Google's own iframe into a container `Element` you hand it) — but the underlying issue looks like a skwasm platform-view compositing bug, not something specific to that package.

## Environment

```
Flutter 3.47.1 • channel stable
Framework revision 6655482ec0 (2026-08-19)
Engine revision 5d5317886
Tools Dart 3.13.1
```
- Platform: web, `--wasm` (skwasm renderer)
- Reproduces in headless Chromium (`--headless=new`) **only** with real GPU acceleration enabled (`--use-gl=angle --use-angle=gl --enable-gpu-rasterization --ignore-gpu-blocklist`). Does **not** reproduce with `--disable-gpu` (software/Skia CPU fallback) — that's the part that makes it easy to miss.
- Reproduces identically whether skwasm is running single-threaded (the default, no cross-origin isolation) or genuinely multi-threaded (page served with `Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin` + `Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: require-corp`, confirmed via `crossOriginIsolated === true` and the single-threaded-fallback console warning disappearing). So it isn't specific to the single-threaded fallback path either.

